color-theme: biotechnology

=== layout: title-slide

# Lecture 06D

## Biotechnology: Business Plans
## Dr. Neil Voss
## Oct 8, 2026

@notes

Adapted from lect06c-business_plan.odp, including its original credit to Elaine Allen.
Modernized using the 2015-2021 sample executive summaries and Fall 2026 project requirements.
Sample company claims are historical student proposals, not validated scientific results.

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Connect a specific problem, biological mechanism, and product.
- Distinguish published evidence from a proposed result.
- Identify the customer, alternatives, and next milestone.
- Build a readable executive summary and a complete business plan.

=== layout: two-panels

# Four terms for your plan

@left

## Customer and value

- [*Customer*]{underline=true}: the person or organization that pays.
- [*Value proposition*]{underline=true}: why that customer would choose your product.

@right

## Evidence and progress

- [*Milestone*]{underline=true}: a measurable result that supports the next decision.
- [*Assumption*]{underline=true}: something your plan relies on that still needs support.

=== layout: section

# From an idea to a credible plan

=== layout: two-panels

# What would make you believe it?

@left

## Proposal

We will grow replacement organs from a patient's own cells.

@right

## Your first questions

- Which tissue, cells, and biological method?
- What has already been demonstrated?
- What must work before a patient could benefit?

@notes

Ask students before showing the next slide. Inspired by the 2015 3DOR,
2017 3D Vive, 2018 Breath of Hope, 2020 3D Prints, and 2021 Sky3D samples.
These are historical student proposals, not evidence of available treatments.

=== layout: one-panel

# A plan connects claims to evidence

@body

| Reader's question | Your answer needs |
| --- | --- |
| Why this problem? | A specific need and supporting evidence |
| Why this product? | A plausible biological mechanism |
| Why this company? | An advantage over existing alternatives |
| What happens next? | Milestones, people, costs, and decisions |

@notes

Replaces the legacy definition and blanket three-to-five-year investment-return claim.
The development timeline must follow the proposed technology and its uncertainties.

=== layout: two-panels

# Two assignments, different scope

@left

## Individual executive summary

- One-page company argument
- Cover and references on separate pages
- Problem, science, product, market, competition, milestones
- Supports your four-minute pitch

@right

## Group business plan

- A deeper, integrated company case
- Executive summary plus full sections
- Management, development, and investment logic
- Figures, tables, and supporting references

@notes

The three-page individual submission format comes from the submission section in LECT06B.
The full group plan is not restricted to three pages. Follow Blackboard for the
announced group formatting rules. Current scope: Fall 2026 PROJECTS.md.

=== layout: reference

# Structure of the full business plan

@body

1. Cover: company, product, team, logo, and table of contents
2. Executive summary: one page that stands on its own
3. Business and technology background: need, mechanism, evidence, IP
4. Market opportunity: customers, competition, strategy, milestones
5. Management team: skills and responsibility for the work
6. Investment summary: funding needs and financial table
7. References; optional appendix for supporting detail

@notes

Preserves the section sequence in legacy business-plan slides 4 and 28.
IP means intellectual property. Describe the evidence behind any protection claim.
An optional appendix does not replace citations in the main document.

=== layout: section

# Write the one-page argument

=== layout: one-panel

# Give each paragraph a job

@body

| Paragraph | Main job |
| --- | --- |
| Problem | Identify who needs help and why |
| Science and product | Explain what you will make and how it works |
| Market and competition | Name the customer and a reason to switch |
| Next step | State the key milestone and remaining uncertainty |

@notes

This is a writing scaffold, not a new required paragraph count or rubric.
The summary must stand alone even when the full plan contains much more detail.
Write the full argument first, then compress it without removing the mechanism.

=== layout: two-panels

# Keep the useful parts of past samples

@left

## Moves worth borrowing

- EnteroDetect: begin with a concrete testing problem.
- Aquazyme: use clear section headings.
- Virona: organize development into successive tests.

@right

## Questions to keep asking

- Does the summary explain the actual mechanism?
- Is each claimed result supported by a source?
- Who pays, and why choose this approach?

@notes

Sources: 2016-EnteroDetect, 2020-Aquazyme, and 2018-Virona executive summaries.
Praise these writing moves without endorsing the samples' scientific or market claims.
The sample collection includes excerpts from longer group plans; its table-of-contents
page numbers are not the current individual assignment's page requirements.

=== layout: two-panels

# Start with the problem you can address

@left

## Too broad

Plastic pollution harms the entire planet.

@right

## More useful

Which plastic, in which waste stream, creates a costly problem for which customer?

Name the material, setting, and person making the purchase decision.

@notes

Inspired by Aquazyme and Biodegradables. This is a prompt for narrowing the problem,
not a claim that a particular waste stream or customer has already been validated.

=== layout: four-panels

# Make the biological mechanism visible

@top-left

## 1. Biological component

Which organism, cell, gene, protein, or pathway?

@top-right

## 2. Intervention

What will you change, produce, deliver, or measure?

@bottom-left

## 3. Product action

How does that change address the stated problem?

@bottom-right

## 4. Evidence

Which result supports this step? What remains unknown?

@notes

Have students sketch arrows linking the four boxes for their own proposal.
This replaces lists of impressive techniques with a causal explanation.

=== layout: two-panels

# Name the hard step

@left

## Nitrogen-fixing crop proposal

The Nifty Genes sample identifies nif genes and nitrogenase.

That specificity gives the reader something to investigate.

@right

## Questions for the plan

- Where must the enzyme function?
- What evidence supports activity in the intended crop?
- What result would justify the next development step?

@notes

Source: 2021-Nifty_Genes_Executive_Summary.pdf, summary page.
The sample moves directly from adding genes to eliminating fertilizer use.
Use that logical gap as a writing exercise; do not present the proposed outcome as established.

=== layout: one-panel

# Separate three kinds of statements

@body

| Status | Useful wording |
| --- | --- |
| Published finding | A cited study demonstrated X in this model. |
| Company proposal | We propose to use X for this specific product. |
| Development target | Our next test would measure Y against this comparator. |

@notes

Several organ-printing samples describe a fully functional transplantable organ and
the elimination of rejection as accomplished facts. These are proposal claims.
The distinction also applies to claimed funding, contracts, patents, and approvals.

=== layout: two-panels

# Turn confidence into a testable claim

@left

## Weak claim

Our product is completely safe and has no competitors.

@right

## Useful revision

We aim to improve a defined outcome compared with a named alternative.

The plan identifies the evidence still needed to assess performance and risk.

@notes

Synthetic example, not a quotation from one student. Motivated by recurrent claims in
the Chronic, FluCream, PurePod, organ-printing, and Lac Locks samples.
Enthusiasm should come from specificity and a plausible next step.

=== layout: one-panel

# Keep the biology central

@body

- Explain what the biological component actually does.
- A device, app, natural extract, or familiar drug needs a clear course-fit argument.
- Name the molecular or cellular biotechnology that makes your product possible.
- Use feedback to refine the proposal before polishing its branding.

@notes

Compare the An Eye on Health and ChildBirth Pain Receptor samples with current course
requirements. A historical sample is not automatic evidence that a new topic fits.
Assess the proposed mechanism, not merely the category or product name.

=== layout: section

# Who buys it, and why?

=== layout: two-panels

# The user and the customer may differ

@left

## User or beneficiary

- Patient receiving a treatment
- Technician running a test
- Community benefiting from cleaner water

@right

## Possible customer

- Hospital or payer
- Testing laboratory or food producer
- Water utility or site owner

@notes

These are possibilities to investigate, not validated customers for a particular proposal.
Ask who approves a purchase and which budget would pay for it.

=== layout: one-panel

# Choose one starting market

@body

- Name one initial product and one customer group.
- Explain where it fits into that customer's workflow.
- Identify a reason to switch and a barrier to switching.
- Show evidence of need; label estimates and assumptions.
- Treat later markets as possible expansion.

@notes

BioLights lists many future lighting products; HYGIENIC lists many surfaces.
Use these samples to discuss why a focused first product makes a plan easier to evaluate.
Retains the legacy deck's niche and commercialization guidance.

=== layout: two-panels

# Competition includes the current solution

@left

## Direct alternative

Another product using a similar approach for the same customer.

@right

## Indirect alternative

A different way to meet the need, including the customer's current practice.

@notes

BioLights discusses other biotechnology lighting companies but discounts conventional
lighting. Ask which alternatives the customer would actually compare at purchase time.
A different technology or country does not automatically remove competition.

=== layout: one-panel

# Compare on the customer's terms

@body

| Option | Performance | Total cost | Practical limit |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Current practice | Cite a result | State the unit | Identify the burden |
| Named competitor | Cite a result | Use the same unit | Explain the tradeoff |
| Your proposal | State a target | Label the estimate | Name the untested step |

@notes

Fill this table with researched information. Empty template cells are prompts, not facts.
Use comparable conditions, dates, units, and customer groups.
Avoid calling a proposed target a measured advantage.

=== layout: two-panels

# Large problem does not equal large sales

@left

## Aquazyme sample

Compares the cost of ocean cleanup with a low estimate for engineering a microbe.

@right

## Missing business questions

- Who would pay for the service?
- What does delivery and operation cost?
- What evidence supports performance at that scale?
- Which costs grow with the amount treated?

@notes

Source: 2020-Aquazyme_Executive_Summary.pdf, Market paragraph.
Do not reuse its numerical estimates as current facts. The teaching issue is the gap
between a one-time laboratory input and the cost of delivering a complete service.

=== layout: one-panel

# Show the arithmetic and assumptions

@body

| Illustrative monthly scenario (USD) | Calculation | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Revenue | 10 customers x 20 units x 50 | 10,000 |
| Variable production costs | 200 units x 30 | 6,000 |
| Remaining before other costs | 10,000 - 6,000 | 4,000 |
| Other operating costs | Assumed for this exercise | 7,000 |
| Operating result | 4,000 - 7,000 | -3,000 |

@notes

All numbers are invented for classroom arithmetic, not a forecast or investment advice.
Revenue is not profit. This simplified example excludes tax, financing, and capital costs.
The Stem Send sample motivates checking consistency among revenue, expenses, and profit.
Ask what changes if only five customers buy the same quantity.

=== layout: section

# Fund a result you can evaluate

=== layout: two-panels

# A milestone ends with evidence

@left

## Activity

Research the product.

Raise money.

Start selling.

@right

## Milestone

Compare performance with a defined control under stated conditions.

Use that result to decide whether to proceed, revise, or stop.

@notes

This is a library-research project. Students propose justified development tests and
criteria; they are not required to conduct laboratory experiments for this assignment.
Retains the legacy business-plan emphasis on feasible milestones and risk reduction.

=== layout: one-panel

# Build a sequence of decisions

@body

| Stage | Evidence needed | Decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanism | A result in the relevant model | Does the proposed action occur? |
| Prototype | Repeatable performance against a comparator | Is the product useful? |
| Practical use | Delivery, production, and user constraints | Can it work in the intended setting? |
| Expansion | Evidence from the initial use case | Is wider use justified? |

@notes

Adapt the stages to the technology. Virona's sample makes successive development steps
visible, but its proposed outcomes and timelines still need independent support.
Do not assume that success in one model guarantees success in the next setting.

=== layout: one-panel

# Put the largest uncertainty on the page

@body

| Uncertainty | What the plan should explain |
| --- | --- |
| Scientific | The result needed to support the mechanism |
| Practical | Manufacturing, stability, delivery, and scale |
| Commercial | Who buys and what makes them switch |
| Responsible use | Risks, containment, and required oversight |

@notes

Aquazyme motivates the distinction between a controlled process and environmental release.
Therapeutic proposals motivate checking safety, delivery, and relevant regulatory sources.
Research the requirements for the actual product and jurisdiction; do not assume approval.

=== layout: two-panels

# Management means responsibility

@left

## Connect a person to a task

- Scientific mechanism and evidence
- Market and competition research
- Cost model and milestones
- Writing, figures, and final review

@right

## Identify missing expertise

- Which milestone needs outside help?
- What skill would an adviser provide?
- Which proposed partners still need agreement?

@notes

Preserves the legacy team-skills and adviser guidance. Describe actual team experience.
Do not invent degrees, partnerships, employees, or a commitment from a real organization.
For the course project, at least two members review each major section.

=== layout: one-panel

# Tie the funding request to milestones

@body

| Milestone | Cost categories | Funding buys |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanism test | Staff time, materials, analysis | Evidence for the core claim |
| Prototype | Development, quality checks, iteration | A reproducible product candidate |
| Practical-use study | Production, delivery, evaluation | Evidence for the intended setting |

@notes

Add justified estimates and timing in the full plan. Include projected revenue and
expenses over the funded period, their assumptions, and a contingency for delays.
Distinguish funding already received from funding proposed or being sought.
Avoid treating a hoped-for grant, partner, sale, or exit as an accomplished fact.

=== layout: two-panels

# Describe an advantage you can support

@left

## Possible sources of advantage

- Better performance on a defined measure
- Easier fit with a customer's workflow
- Lower total cost under stated assumptions
- A defensible method or capability

@right

## Evidence to provide

- A fair comparison
- Sources for existing technology
- A realistic development plan
- Clear status of intellectual-property claims

@notes

Retains the legacy core-competency and intellectual-property guidance.
Do not label the company patent-pending or protected merely because the idea is new to you.
Patentability and permission to commercialize are research questions, not promises here.

=== layout: section

# Make the document easy to trust

=== layout: one-panel

# Put sources beside the claims they support

@body

- Cite important scientific, market, and quantitative claims.
- Read the underlying study before describing its result.
- Keep the species, model, conditions, date, and units clear.
- Check that each citation supports the sentence using it.
- Use a consistent reference list with working links or DOIs.

@notes

The original deck asks for citations in its appendix slide. Make citation use explicit
throughout the document. A news headline or company claim may identify a lead to investigate;
it does not replace the underlying evidence for a scientific result.

=== layout: two-panels

# Revise for specificity

@left

## Words that need support

- Revolutionary
- Completely safe
- No competition
- Guaranteed profit
- Works for everyone

@right

## Replace with useful information

- Biological component and action
- Conditions tested and limitations
- Named alternatives and tradeoffs
- Assumptions and arithmetic
- Initial customer and use case

@notes

AI-assisted text needs the same scrutiny. Verify citations, preserve your own reasoning,
and follow the course academic-integrity policy. Do not present invented data as evidence.

=== layout: one-panel

# Make one company voice

@body

- Agree on the product, customer, and scientific strategy.
- Use the same names, units, assumptions, and timeline throughout.
- Have at least two people review each major section.
- Check that the summary matches the complete plan.
- Inspect the uploaded file for page breaks, figures, and references.

@notes

Drawn from the current group-project teamwork requirements and legacy formatting guidance.
The summary should not promise a result that the scientific section still treats as unknown.

=== layout: two-panels

# Your turn: test your opening

@left

## Write three sentences

1. This customer has this specific problem.
2. Our product uses this biological mechanism.
3. The next evidence we need is this result.

@right

## Exchange with a classmate

- Underline the biological mechanism.
- Circle the customer.
- Mark one unsupported claim.
- Ask one question that would improve the plan.

@notes

Allow a short writing pause, then discuss one volunteer example.
Students can use their One Great Idea proposal. This exercise is practice, not a new submission.

=== layout: reference

# Check before submitting

@body

- *Problem*: specific need, affected group, and evidence
- *Science*: biological mechanism and its limitations
- *Product*: what the customer receives and how it helps
- *Market*: customer, competition, niche, and business model
- *Next step*: measurable milestones and justified assumptions
- *Full plan*: management, investment table, and supporting sections
- *Document*: readable format, checked citations, and correct uploaded file

